1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,894
In this town, there was a shop that

2
00:00:01,894 --> 00:00:03,511
sold second chances.

3
00:00:03,511 --> 00:00:05,580
I found out too late what they cost.

4
00:00:08,048 --> 00:00:09,995
It started the day someone spread a lie

5
00:00:09,995 --> 00:00:12,144
about my best friend, Lucas.

6
00:00:12,144 --> 00:00:14,016
Our whole class laughed.

7
00:00:14,016 --> 00:00:15,207
I knew the truth.

8
00:00:15,207 --> 00:00:17,324
Lucas looked straight at me, waiting for me

9
00:00:17,324 --> 00:00:18,346
to say something.

10
00:00:18,346 --> 00:00:18,936
I didn't.

11
00:00:23,048 --> 00:00:25,271
After school, he walked away alone.

12
00:00:25,271 --> 00:00:27,378
I tried calling, but he didn't answer.

13
00:00:27,378 --> 00:00:29,378
That's when I noticed a little shop squeezed

14
00:00:29,378 --> 00:00:31,271
between two buildings that had never been

15
00:00:31,271 --> 00:00:32,021
there before.

16
00:00:34,048 --> 00:00:36,388
The sign read, "SECOND CHANCES."

17
00:00:36,388 --> 00:00:39,314
Underneath, in tiny letters: "Refunds cost

18
00:00:39,314 --> 00:00:41,165
more." I stepped inside.

19
00:00:41,165 --> 00:00:43,388
A wall of clocks ticked backward, and an

20
00:00:43,388 --> 00:00:45,559
old shopkeeper asked, "Which day would you

21
00:00:45,559 --> 00:00:46,234
change?"

22
00:00:48,048 --> 00:00:49,527
I told him about Lucas.

23
00:00:49,527 --> 00:00:51,346
He handed me a golden watch.

24
00:00:51,346 --> 00:00:53,824
"Turn it once, and you can redo today,"

25
00:00:53,824 --> 00:00:55,793
he said. "But the shop takes a memory

26
00:00:55,793 --> 00:00:58,112
you love." I thought one memory was worth

27
00:00:58,112 --> 00:00:59,489
saving my best friend.

28
00:01:05,048 --> 00:01:06,250
I turned the watch.

29
00:01:06,250 --> 00:01:08,378
Suddenly I was back in class, hearing the

30
00:01:08,378 --> 00:01:09,856
same lie again.

31
00:01:09,856 --> 00:01:11,399
This time, I stood up.

32
00:01:11,399 --> 00:01:13,367
I told everyone it wasn't true.

33
00:01:13,367 --> 00:01:15,707
Lucas stared at me, then smiled like the

34
00:01:15,707 --> 00:01:16,468
old days.

35
00:01:23,048 --> 00:01:25,154
Walking home, Lucas asked, "Remember the

36
00:01:25,154 --> 00:01:26,867
treehouse we built by the river?"

37
00:01:26,867 --> 00:01:28,133
I tried to picture it.

38
00:01:28,133 --> 00:01:30,888
Nothing. Not the ladder, not the silly sign

39
00:01:30,888 --> 00:01:33,452
we painted. Ten summers of friendship, and one

40
00:01:33,452 --> 00:01:35,298
of my favorite days was gone.

41
00:01:39,048 --> 00:01:40,410
I ran back to the shop.

42
00:01:40,410 --> 00:01:42,665
"Give me that memory back," I begged.

43
00:01:42,665 --> 00:01:44,803
The shopkeeper pointed to the tiny writing on

44
00:01:44,803 --> 00:01:47,761
the sign. "Undoing a second chance costs more

45
00:01:47,761 --> 00:01:48,941
than buying one."

46
00:01:48,941 --> 00:01:50,245
I left empty-handed.

47
00:01:54,048 --> 00:01:55,824
The next morning, Lucas showed up at my

48
00:01:55,824 --> 00:01:57,548
door with an old photograph.

49
00:01:57,548 --> 00:01:59,516
Two kids grinning in a crooked wooden

50
00:01:59,516 --> 00:02:02,080
treehouse. When I took it, the memory rushed

51
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:04,718
back. Lucas blinked, as if he'd forgotten why

52
00:02:04,718 --> 00:02:05,447
he was there.

53
00:02:10,048 --> 00:02:11,282
I was about to thank him when he

54
00:02:11,282 --> 00:02:13,771
asked, "Sorry... what's your name again?"

55
00:02:13,771 --> 00:02:16,069
I laughed, until I saw the golden watch

56
00:02:16,069 --> 00:02:16,910
in his pocket.

57
00:02:16,910 --> 00:02:17,989
He wasn't joking.

58
00:02:23,048 --> 00:02:24,335
I raced to the shop.

59
00:02:24,335 --> 00:02:26,335
The old man finally told me what Lucas

60
00:02:26,335 --> 00:02:28,612
paid. Not just one afternoon.

61
00:02:28,612 --> 00:02:30,644
He gave up every memory we had made

62
00:02:30,644 --> 00:02:33,090
together. He'd traded knowing me so I could

63
00:02:33,090 --> 00:02:33,851
remember him.

64
00:02:38,048 --> 00:02:40,590
I found Lucas at our old treehouse, staring

65
00:02:40,590 --> 00:02:42,144
at the ladder like he didn't know why

66
00:02:42,144 --> 00:02:44,112
he'd come. I sat beside him.

67
00:02:44,112 --> 00:02:45,293
"Hey," I said.

68
00:02:45,293 --> 00:02:46,931
"I'm Noah." He smiled.

69
00:02:46,931 --> 00:02:49,420
"I'm Lucas." And somehow, that felt like a

70
00:02:49,420 --> 00:02:51,053
second chance worth keeping.
